WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.819
High-quality detections help analysts focus their attention.

00:00:02.819 --> 00:00:07.093
Poorly tuned rules may generate excessive false positives, consume investigation time,

00:00:07.093 --> 00:00:09.812
and erode confidence in the detection program.

00:00:09.812 --> 00:00:13.697
Detection authors should consider expected activity, environmental context, baseline patterns,

00:00:13.697 --> 00:00:17.582
known administrative workflows, and the operational burden created by alerts.

00:00:17.582 --> 00:00:23.021
A rule that fires constantly may hide the activity it was meant to reveal.

00:00:23.021 --> 00:00:25.352
Good detections balance sensitivity and specificity.

00:00:25.352 --> 00:00:27.682
Sensitivity helps catch more relevant activity.

00:00:27.682 --> 00:00:29.625
Specificity helps reduce irrelevant matches.

00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:33.510
The right balance depends on the risk, the available telemetry,

00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.395
and what analysts are expected to do with the signal.

00:00:37.395 --> 00:00:40.114
Noise reduction should not mean hiding uncertainty.

00:00:40.114 --> 00:00:43.610
Some detections are intentionally broad because the behavior matters.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.884
In those cases, the rule should include context, enrichment, severity guidance,

00:00:47.884 --> 00:00:51.380
and triage notes so analysts can make faster decisions.
